    <TIER LINGUISTIC_TYPE_REF="default-lt" TIER_ID="default"/>
    <TIER LINGUISTIC_TYPE_REF="imported-sep" TIER_ID="SPEAKER_00">
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a2"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts2" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts27">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>Tons of studies show that public speaking is a major cause for anxiety for many people. At the same time, good public speaking can help you accelerate your career. So, today we're going to give you three killer tips to help you overcome any public speaking anxiety you have. And I have a special guest with us today to help us do that.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a3"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts33" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts37">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>We are here today with my friend Kit Pang. How are you doing today, Kit?</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a392"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts43" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts96">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>So wonderful to have you back on the channel. So, let me tell you a little bit about Kit if you have not met him yet. He is the founder of Boston Speaks, he is a three-time coach for TEDx speakers. He has lots of high-profile clients in the Boston area and beyond. And he recently ran a public speaking summit that was focused entirely on helping speakers like you overcome your anxiety. I was one of 30 guest speakers that participated in that. And by the way, you can still access a lot of that content for free. I will put a link in the description below that you can take a look at when we're done watching this video. So, Kit, let's talk about the three big takeaways that you received that you've gathered from this public speaking summit. So, the first question is this: What is the cause of most of our public speaking in the first place?</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a6"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts167" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts187">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>That's exactly right. I've experienced the same thing. The emotions start to fade away, but then when you start thinking those thoughts again, it makes the anxiety surge up again. So, if it's our mindset, how do we get to the bottom of what's making us tick inside? How do we explore that more?</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a10"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts331" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts405">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>Right. I love that because so often people will talk about my voice trembles. Well, that's just a symptom of what's happening in your head. And if your voice is trembling, why is it trembling? And you trace those thoughts down, keep drilling down until you get to the root, which is if I make a mistake, if I'm not flawless or something like that, right? Then I won't seem competent in front of my bosses. That will, if you, if that's what you're thinking, you're going to get nervous because you have just raised the stakes on your own and put more pressure on yourself. But what I'm hearing is when this client was, this speaker was talking to her team, it wasn't about that, right? She just wanted to be authentic and be herself. And then, of course, she probably came across much better when she had that mindset. So that's fantastic. By the way, just another reminder for you viewer, if you want to see the public speaking summit content that Kit has assembled, there's a link to that in the description below the video. Let's go through another example of digging down into the why, why people are anxious when they don't really need to be. Do you have another example from a speaker?</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a12"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts525" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts577">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>No, that's great. I believe that we all need role models. And if we can hear that people who have gone before us have conquered this and managed this successfully, it's really inspirational to hear them overcome that. So, just to recap, before we get to the third tip, and this is really a sequence of three steps: the first thing, you have to realize that your inner world is driving your anxiety, your mindset, your thought patterns, and is pushing those emotions. Secondly, you have to drill down by asking good questions: why, why, why, until you get down to the root cause, that belief that triggers your anxiety all the more. So, now we get to the third tip, and that is: how can we go about starting to improve our mindset so that we can overcome those limitations?</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a15"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts631" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts700">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>That's great. You know, one of the things as you're talking, I'm thinking about, well, what's my mindset? How did I get over it? Because I don't, I get a little nervous, but just the normal amount. Anxiety does not hold me back from public speaking anymore. And one of the decisions I made, the new thought I have that I adopted a while back was: this is all just practice. I view every single public speaking situation I've ever been in as practice for the next one. I have been right in front of clients, high-paying clients, and I still say to myself, it's just practice. It's just practice. It's just, and so the fact that it's practice relaxes me because I practice my public speaking a ton. It's just one more practice. And it really frees me then to improvise a little bit, to be myself, to be spontaneous and let my personality shine through rather than letting that anxiety come through. So I just want to remind the viewer one more time that you can access a lot of that content for free from Kit's Public Speaking Summit on Anxiety and gaining more confidence. And Kit, did you want to give me a quick description of that before we sign off?</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a18"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts715" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts725">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>Wonderful. There's a link below. Like I mentioned, Kit, thanks for coming back on the channel and spending some time with us today.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a20"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts735" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts743">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>See you all later. Take care. Bye-bye.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
    </TIER>
