    <TIER LINGUISTIC_TYPE_REF="default-lt" TIER_ID="SPEAKER_01">
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a391"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts38" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts46">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>Alex, I'm so glad to be here. Thank you so much.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a393"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts98" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts166">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>Yeah. And the cost, let's drill down to the answer right now. It's basically how we think about the situation that's in front of us. Basically saying our mindset. I just want to give a quick reasoning into that. Now, I'm not sure if you are all aware, but your emotions only last for 90 seconds. So that means within the 90 seconds, your body is releasing all these chemicals, adrenaline. And then after 90 seconds, the question is, "why does the emotion keep on coming back?" In our case, the nerves or the anxiety. It's because we are thinking of the same thing such as, oh, my voice is trembling again. I'm such a bad speaker. I'm nervous. So have you ever spoke before? Many people say this. And I asked them, do you get nervous? Kind of like when you get in a minute, two minutes, three minutes in after your talk. And most people say, no, I got into a rhythm. I feel good. It's usually that buildup, right? So again, your emotions only last for 90 seconds. So the question is, why? It's your thoughts, your mindset. We keep on having these thoughts over and over and over again. That's why we just keep on feeling it over and over again.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a394"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts190" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts316">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>Yeah, and I'll give us a good exercise to do that first, but I just want to quickly explain when it comes to our mindset. If any of you have seen the iceberg picture example before, like you can see, you know, what's on top of the iceberg, and then there's a lot of things that you can't really see under the iceberg. So, when it comes to the thought side of things, it's really: do you know what you're conscious that you're thinking about? For example, people might say, My voice is trembling. Oh, it's trembling again. But there's usually a deeper thought, which we call a belief that you're usually not aware of. So, just as a quick example of one of my clients, we actually use this exercise. It's called the five whys. They use this in business a lot. So, basically, once you know what kind of thoughts that you have, you drill that thought down with more questions as in, why do I feel this way? What does that mean? And then you can ask more and more and more because when you ask it more, you drill down to a more deep-rooted thought. So, that example of one of my clients is basically whenever she speaks to higher-ups, she feels like she always has to give the right answer and give value. Okay. But whenever she talks to people that she manages, her co-workers, people that sees every single day, she's fine just being herself because it's not as high stakes. So, what we did with the five "why" exercise is basically: if we know the first thought, as in, as in, oh, I feel so nervous. I always, you know, I have to be, I have to always up my game when I'm speaking to executives because the stakes are high. And then, and I ask, why do you feel like you always have to be at the top of your game? And then she would answer something like, Well, if I don't give value, people will look down on me. People will see maybe that I'm incompetent. And then I would, here's another question that you can do on your own. So, when you ask another question, you want to ask another question based off of what you said. So, another question I could ask would be: so, are you saying if you don't know what you're talking about, that you will seem incompetent?</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a396"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts318" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts334">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>And then, you know, we can go dig down and dig down and dig down and dig down further. But that's a quick example of the five why, five question exercise to what we're doing with the mindset.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a397"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts408" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts528">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>Yes. And so this other client that I work with, here's the thing. He was at some event a long time ago and he saw another speaker. Okay. And this other speaker to him was a nervous wreck. I'm not sure if you've seen people in real life or on YouTube or other videos. This guy was a nervous wreck. And because he saw someone else really just, you know, fidgeting, you know, sweating, really nervous, to him, he said, man, I never want to come away like that. Okay. And so just by seeing other people being nervous, or actually just hearing other people say, hey, being nervous is bad, we conceptualize that and then reinternalize that ourselves. Right. And so we've created a meaning of our own of, man, we can never be seen as nervous like that. But it all goes back to Alex. I just want to wrap up kind of the things that we just talked about, right? One, it's all about your mindset, how are you thinking about your speaking? Second, do you know more of the deep-rooted thoughts? And so to use this example, the person is basically saying disbelief. If I'm seen as nervous or someone else sees someone else as nervous, that means being nervous is a bad thing. Now, some of you might be thinking, kid, of course, being nervous is a bad thing. That is under the iceberg. That is the subconscious. How do I know when someone is free from speaking anxiety? We're able to work through all of the beliefs that's causing speaking anxiety in the first place, just like that one. Being seen as nervous is a bad thing. And just a quick thing: you know, you were mentioning the Fearless Public Speaking Summit. The reason, the main reason we're having that summit is because if you want to be a better speaker, we are stuck usually with what? Our own mindset. And so we have 30 amazing speakers, Alex himself and other great speakers, showing you about how they think about their nerves and about their speaking. So that's one of the main reasons why we have that summit in the first place. But I'm going on here, Alex, give it back to you.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a398"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts581" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts618">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>To improve your mindset, this is something that took me a while to really grasp the concept of, is to really understand the way that we are looking at a situation is only a way, it's only our way that we're used to looking at the situation. The key is knowing how to keep an open mind. Now, when I say that, many of you might think, kid, I have an open mind already. But it's learning how to have an open mind and look at all different perceptions of it. There's a great quote that I want to share with you, Alex, and the rest of the listeners. I forgot who said this. I read this online somewhere. And the person said, your mind is like a parachute. If it's not open, it doesn't work.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a399"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts621" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts630">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>Being able to see different perspectives about the situation that you're in will set you free from speaking anxiety. It's really as simple as that.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a400"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts702" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts714">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>Yeah, so the Fearless Public Speaking Summit is all about helping you become a more confident speaker and knowing how to break free from speaking anxiety by understanding the mindsets and strategies of some of the best speakers in the world.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
        <ANNOTATION>
            <ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION ANNOTATION_ID="a401"
                TIME_SLOT_REF1="ts728" TIME_SLOT_REF2="ts738">
                <ANNOTATION_VALUE>Alex, thank you so much. Pleasure being here. Thank you so much.</ANNOTATION_VALUE>
            </ALIGNABLE_ANNOTATION>
        </ANNOTATION>
    </TIER>
